5.	What is the purpose of cron in Linux?
In Linux, cron is a time-based job scheduler used to automatically execute tasks (scripts, commands, or programs) at specified intervals or times.
 
Purpose of cron:
1.	Automate repetitive tasks
o	Run backups, clean temporary files, or update software automatically.
2.	Schedule tasks at specific times
o	Daily, weekly, monthly, or even every minute.
3.	Maintain system administration
o	Tasks like log rotation, system monitoring, or sending reports can be automated.
4.	Run scripts in the background without user intervention
o	Cron jobs run silently at scheduled times.
 
How it works:
•	Cron uses a file called crontab to store scheduled tasks.
•	Each line in crontab specifies when and what to execute.
Crontab format:
* * * * * command_to_run
- - - - -
| | | | |
| | | | +---- Day of the week (0-7, Sunday=0 or 7)
| | | +------ Month (1-12)
| | +-------- Day of the month (1-31)
| +---------- Hour (0-23)
+------------ Minute (0-59)
Example:
0	5 * * * /home/user/backup.sh

