9. How can you log the output of a script using the tee command?
Basic Syntax:
command | tee filename
•	command → any command or script output
•	filename → file to store the output
•	By default, tee overwrites the file.
•	Use -a to append to the file instead of overwriting:
command | tee -a filename
 
Example 1: Logging script output
#!/bin/bash
# Script to display date and log it

echo "Script started at $(date)" | tee script.log
echo "Disk usage:" | tee -a script.log
df -h | tee -a script.log
echo "Script ended at $(date)" | tee -a script.log
Explanation:
•	tee script.log → saves output to script.log and shows on screen.
•	tee -a script.log → appends subsequent outputs to the same log file.
 
Example 2: Running a script and logging externally
./my_script.sh | tee output.log
•	Logs all output of my_script.sh to output.log while displaying it on the terminal.
